WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:05.740
FELIPE HOFFA: Data in BigQuery
is always encrypted at rest

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.500
by default. But some projects
may have a requirement

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.450
to manage its own keys.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.930
On Cloud Key Management Service,
I just gave BigQuery access

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.760
to read one of my own keys.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.470
And I copied that key's
ID to use in the query.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.700
[INAUDIBLE] BigQuery.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.050
When I choose a destination
table for a query,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.930
I can choose which
encryption type to use.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:28.930
To use my own keys, I can select
Customer Managed Encryption.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.980
And I can give it the ID of
the key we just got from KMS.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.080
The [INAUDIBLE] files
show that this new table

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.850
is encrypted with my key.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.890
Because a key
lives in Cloud KMS,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.810
I can query this
tablet as usual.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.070
Let's try that.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.580
As long as I have access to this
key, my existing [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.850
will run unaffected.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.410
And this is how you can easily
encrypt data stored in BigQuery

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.980
using your own keys.

